exercises.solution:
One word of change turns the one-level version into a recursive one:

```python
def flatten(nested):
    result = []
    for item in nested:
        if isinstance(item, list):
            result.extend(flatten(item))   # trust the recursion for the sub-list
        else:
            result.append(item)             # base case: a number
    return result
```

The termination measure is the nesting depth: each recursive call is on a strictly smaller piece of the structure. Correctness is an induction on depth: assuming `flatten` works on the (shallower) sub-lists, this loop puts their numbers in the right order. The total work is proportional to the number of numbers plus the number of lists, since each is visited once.

